// §5.10.3.4: "The absdelta() event function enables efficient and accurate
// sampling of analog signals for use in digital behavioral code. ... This
// function is only allowed in an initial or always block of a Verilog-AMS
// module." The clause's own example is `electrical_sampler`, an
// `always @(absdelta(V(e_in), vdelta, ttol, vtol)) sampled = V(e_in);`.
//
// TWO CLAIMS, BOTH LATITUDE-FREE, because most of this clause is not. The
// clause grants the simulator an explicit window — "The simulator is allowed to
// schedule this event at any time between the time corresponding to the
// interpolated absolute change of (delta - expr_tol) and ... (delta +
// expr_tol)" — and also lets it choose the tolerances when they are omitted or
// zero. Nothing about WHEN an event fires can therefore be asserted to a
// number. What can be asserted is the pair of statements the clause makes
// without a window:
//
//   1. delta = 0. "If delta is set to zero, an event is generated every
//      timestep the expression value changes with zero tolerances. expr_tol and
//      time_tol, if specified, will have no effect and be ignored." An event on
//      every timestep means the sampled copy IS the signal, at every analog
//      point the run reports.
//
//   2. any delta. The whole purpose of the function is that the held sample
//      never drifts from expr by more than delta — "When the expr value changes
//      in absolute value by more than delta, relative to the previous
//      absdelta() event" a new event is generated. So |held - V(e_in)| <= delta
//      is a hard bound, and the window above only moves WHEN the correction
//      happens, never whether the bound is kept. Plus: an event is generated
//      "During initialization or dc sweep analysis", so the bound holds from
//      t = 0 and not only after the first change.
//
// DERIVATION. V(e_in) is a linear ramp, 0 V at t = 0 rising to 2 V at t = 20n,
// i.e. V(e_in) = $abstime * 1e8 volts exactly at every listed point:
//
//   //! time  0     5n    10n   15n   20n
//   //! wave  0.0   0.5   1.0   1.5   2.0
//
//   `tracked` (delta = 0):    an event every timestep, so the copy follows the
//                             signal; the residual `tracked - $abstime*1e8` is
//                             bounded by one reported step.
//   `held`  (delta = 0.3):    the ramp moves 0.5 V between listed points, which
//                             is MORE than 0.3, so at least one event must fall
//                             in every interval; whatever instant the simulator
//                             picks inside its window, the gap to V(e_in) stays
//                             near 0.3.
//
// WHY delta IS 0.3 AND NOT 0.5. An earlier draft used 0.5 — exactly the step
// the ramp takes between reported points — and §5.10.3.4 fires only when the
// value changes "by MORE than delta". |0.5 − 0.0| is not more than 0.5, so a
// literally conforming implementation would have generated no event after
// initialization at all, left `held` at 0.0, and failed the second check by 2.0
// at t = 20n. The fixture would have passed only an implementation that used
// `>=` — it tested for a bug. 0.3 is strictly below the step, so the rule is
// exercised on every interval under either reading.
//
// WHY THE BOUNDS CARRY SLACK. The clause's scheduling window means the
// correction may land slightly after the crossing, so the reachable gap is
// delta plus whatever that window costs, not delta exactly. 0.5 on claim 2 is
// 0.3 + 0.2 of window. A tool that sampled `held` only once at t = 0 still
// drifts to a gap of 2.0 by t = 20n, four times the bound, so the assertion
// keeps its teeth.
//
// CLAIM 1'S BOUND IS ONE STEP, NOT ZERO, AND THAT IS AN M02 DEPENDENCY.
// Asserting `tracked == V(e_in)` to float slack would additionally assert that a
// value written by a digital `always` is visible to the analog block at the SAME
// instant — the zero-delay A2D/D2A boundary question that this row's SPEC.md
// explicitly defers to M02. Until M02 settles it, the honest bound is one
// reported step (0.5) plus window (0.1). A tool that treated delta = 0 as "no
// events" leaves `tracked` at the §3.2 default 0.0 and still fails from t = 10n
// onward. When M02 lands, tighten this to equality at 1e-12 — the LRM's rule
// here really is equality, and this fixture is currently under-claiming it.
//
// WHAT FAILS TODAY, in the order the compiler reaches it — re-captured after
// review, which found the two reversed here. FIRST `error[E0205]: unsupported
// module item: found `always``, on line 95: `src/frontend/parser.zig:1265`
// refuses `always` outside a digital run, so the only call site §5.10.3.4
// permits is not a legal module item at all. BEHIND it sits `error[E0513]:
// absdelta() is not implemented`, VerA's own diagnostic title. The existing
// fixture `tests/fixtures/ch05_analog_behavior/absdelta_digital_only.va` puts
// the call in an `analog` block, where §5.10.3.4 forbids it, and so pins the
// placement rule by way of a capability message; this file is the legal call
// site.
//
//! lrm 5.10.3.4
//! lrm 7.3.4
//! analysis tran
//! time 0, 5n, 10n, 15n, 20n
//! wave V(e_in) = 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0
`timescale 1ns/100ps
`include "check.vh"
module m01_absdelta_sampler(e_in, p);
  inout e_in, p;
  electrical e_in, p;
  real tracked, held;

  always @(absdelta(V(e_in), 0.0))
    tracked = V(e_in);

  always @(absdelta(V(e_in), 0.3))
    held = V(e_in);

  analog begin
    // One reported step (0.5) + window (0.1). Tighten to 1e-12 once M02 settles
    // same-instant visibility; see CLAIM 1'S BOUND above.
    `CHECK("absdelta with delta = 0 samples every timestep, so the copy follows the signal", tracked - $abstime * 1e8, 0.0, 0.6);
    // delta (0.3) + scheduling window (0.2). delta is deliberately below the
    // 0.5 step so the "more than delta" rule fires on every interval.
    `CHECK("absdelta with delta = 0.3 never lets the held sample drift past 0.5 V", held - V(e_in), 0.0, 0.5);
    I(p) <+ 1m * tracked;
  end
endmodule
